  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 219 AM EDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nThe main takeaways of the long-term period are a warm-up, plus \nrain chances returning on Friday and Saturday.\n\nOur current dry airmass will remain in place through Wednesday. \nIn fact, relative humidity may fall to a near-critical threshold \nfor hazardous fire weather conditions on Tuesday afternoon. \nWednesday and Thursday will bring an amplifying subtropical ridge \nover the Southeast. Subsidence associated with this area of high \npressure and continued dry air will make for essentially zero PoPs\nthrough Thursday. Meanwhile, high temperatures will return to the\nupper 80s and lower 90s (outside of the mountains, where highs in\nthe 70s are forecasted). Low temperatures will return to the \nmid-60s to upper 60s essentially area-wide.\n\nMoisture is progged to increase on Friday and Saturday as the \nsubtropical high weakens/shifts eastward and low-/mid-level flow \noff of the Gulf and Atlantic spreads inland. This setup will bring\nthe return of isolated to scattered showers and storms both days.\nThe synoptic pattern at the end of the period and AI NWP \nConvective Hazard Forecasts from NCAR lack signals for severe \nweather potential. Rain could moderate the temperatures across \nparts of the area and keep highs in the lower 80s to mid-80s both \ndays.",
